@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "GROUP BY - SQL Server";
}

<div class="container-fluid py-4">

    <!-- =========================
         PAGE HEADER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="mb-5">

        <h1 class="fw-bold">
            GROUP BY
        </h1>

        <p class="lead text-muted">
            The GROUP BY clause is used to group rows that have the same
            values in one or more columns.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         1. INTRODUCTION
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What is GROUP BY?
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>GROUP BY</strong> clause in SQL Server is used
            to arrange rows into groups based on one or more columns.
        </p>

        <p>
            It is mainly used with aggregate functions such as
            <strong>COUNT()</strong>, <strong>SUM()</strong>,
            <strong>AVG()</strong>, <strong>MIN()</strong> and
            <strong>MAX()</strong>.
        </p>

        <div class="alert alert-info">

            <strong>In simple words:</strong>

            <p class="mb-0 mt-2">
                GROUP BY takes similar records and puts them into
                logical groups so that we can perform calculations
                on each group.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         2. WHY GROUP BY
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Why Do We Use GROUP BY?
        </h2>

        <p>
            GROUP BY is useful when we want summarized information
            instead of displaying every individual row.
        </p>

        <ul>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Find the number of employees in each department.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Calculate total salary department-wise.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Calculate average salary for each department.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Find minimum and maximum salary in each department.
            </li>

            <li>
                Generate summary reports from large tables.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         3. SYNTAX
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            GROUP BY Syntax
        </h2>

        <pre class="bg-light p-4 rounded border"><code>SELECT column_name, aggregate_function(column_name)
FROM table_name
GROUP BY column_name;</code></pre>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         4. SAMPLE TABLE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Example Table
        </h2>

        <p>
            Consider the following <strong>Employees</strong> table:
        </p>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>EmployeeId</th>
                        <th>EmployeeName</th>
                        <th>Department</th>
                        <th>Salary</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>1</td>
                        <td>Rahul</td>
                        <td>IT</td>
                        <td>50000</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>2</td>
                        <td>Priya</td>
                        <td>HR</td>
                        <td>40000</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>3</td>
                        <td>Amit</td>
                        <td>IT</td>
                        <td>60000</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>4</td>
                        <td>Neha</td>
                        <td>HR</td>
                        <td>45000</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>5</td>
                        <td>Rohit</td>
                        <td>Sales</td>
                        <td>35000</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         5. BASIC GROUP BY
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Basic GROUP BY Example
        </h2>

        <p>
            Suppose we want to find the number of employees
            in each department.
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-light p-4 rounded border"><code>SELECT Department,
       COUNT(*) AS EmployeeCount
FROM Employees
GROUP BY Department;</code></pre>

        <h4 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Output
        </h4>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered">

                <thead class="table-primary">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Department</th>
                        <th>EmployeeCount</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>HR</td>
                        <td>2</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>IT</td>
                        <td>2</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Sales</td>
                        <td>1</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         6. HOW GROUP BY WORKS
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            How GROUP BY Works
        </h2>

        <p>
            In the above example, SQL Server looks at the
            <strong>Department</strong> column and creates groups
            for matching values.
        </p>

        <div class="alert alert-secondary">

            <p class="mb-2">
                <strong>IT Group:</strong>
                Rahul, Amit
            </p>

            <p class="mb-2">
                <strong>HR Group:</strong>
                Priya, Neha
            </p>

            <p class="mb-0">
                <strong>Sales Group:</strong>
                Rohit
            </p>

        </div>

        <p>
            Then <strong>COUNT()</strong> calculates the number
            of records in every group.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         7. GROUP BY WITH SUM
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            GROUP BY with SUM()
        </h2>

        <p>
            We can use GROUP BY with SUM() to calculate the
            total salary for every department.
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-light p-4 rounded border"><code>SELECT Department,
       SUM(Salary) AS TotalSalary
FROM Employees
GROUP BY Department;</code></pre>

        <h4 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Result
        </h4>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered">

                <thead class="table-success">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Department</th>
                        <th>TotalSalary</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>HR</td>
                        <td>85000</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>IT</td>
                        <td>110000</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Sales</td>
                        <td>35000</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         8. GROUP BY WITH AVG
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            GROUP BY with AVG()
        </h2>

        <p>
            AVG() calculates the average salary for every department.
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-light p-4 rounded border"><code>SELECT Department,
       AVG(Salary) AS AverageSalary
FROM Employees
GROUP BY Department;</code></pre>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         9. GROUP BY WITH MIN
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            GROUP BY with MIN()
        </h2>

        <p>
            MIN() returns the lowest salary in each department.
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-light p-4 rounded border"><code>SELECT Department,
       MIN(Salary) AS MinimumSalary
FROM Employees
GROUP BY Department;</code></pre>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         10. GROUP BY WITH MAX
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            GROUP BY with MAX()
        </h2>

        <p>
            MAX() returns the highest salary in each department.
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-light p-4 rounded border"><code>SELECT Department,
       MAX(Salary) AS MaximumSalary
FROM Employees
GROUP BY Department;</code></pre>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         11. MULTIPLE FUNCTIONS
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            GROUP BY with Multiple Aggregate Functions
        </h2>

        <p>
            We can use multiple aggregate functions in the same query.
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-light p-4 rounded border"><code>SELECT
    Department,
    COUNT(*) AS EmployeeCount,
    SUM(Salary) AS TotalSalary,
    AVG(Salary) AS AverageSalary,
    MIN(Salary) AS MinimumSalary,
    MAX(Salary) AS MaximumSalary
FROM Employees
GROUP BY Department;</code></pre>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         12. GROUP BY WITH HAVING
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            GROUP BY with HAVING
        </h2>

        <p>
            The HAVING clause is used to filter groups after
            GROUP BY has been applied.
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-light p-4 rounded border"><code>SELECT Department,
       COUNT(*) AS EmployeeCount
FROM Employees
GROUP BY Department
HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1;</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-warning mt-3">

            <strong>Remember:</strong>

            <p class="mb-0 mt-2">
                WHERE filters individual rows, while HAVING filters
                grouped results.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         13. WHERE VS HAVING
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            WHERE vs HAVING
        </h2>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>WHERE</th>
                        <th>HAVING</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Filters individual rows.</td>
                        <td>Filters groups.</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Used before GROUP BY.</td>
                        <td>Used after GROUP BY.</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <code>WHERE Salary &gt; 40000</code>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         14. IMPORTANT RULE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-danger">

            <h3 class="fw-bold">
                Important GROUP BY Rule
            </h3>

            <p>
                When a column is selected without an aggregate function,
                that column should normally be included in the GROUP BY
                clause.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-white p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT Department,
       COUNT(*) AS EmployeeCount
FROM Employees
GROUP BY Department;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         15. REAL WORLD USE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Real-World Uses of GROUP BY
        </h2>

        <ul>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Department-wise employee reports.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                City-wise customer reports.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Product-category sales reports.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Monthly sales summaries.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Branch-wise transaction reports.
            </li>

            <li>
                Category-wise product analysis.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         16. IMPORTANT POINTS
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-info">

            <h3 class="fw-bold">
                Important Points
            </h3>

            <ul class="mb-0">

                <li class="mb-2">
                    GROUP BY groups rows with the same values.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    It is commonly used with aggregate functions.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    COUNT(), SUM(), AVG(), MIN() and MAX()
                    are common aggregate functions.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    HAVING is used to filter groups.
                </li>

                <li>
                    GROUP BY is very useful for reporting and
                    data analysis.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         17. SUMMARY
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-success">

            <h3 class="fw-bold">
                Summary
            </h3>

            <p class="mb-0">
                GROUP BY is used to divide rows into groups based
                on common values. It is mainly used with aggregate
                functions to generate summarized information.
                GROUP BY is an important SQL Server concept used
                extensively in reports, dashboards and data analysis.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


